<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battenberg family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Battenberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battenberg family was a morganatic branch of the grand ducal house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, founded when Prince Alexander of Hesse married Countess Julia Hauke, who was created Princess of Battenberg in 1858. Their sons included Alexander, the first prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and Louis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sea-lord">First Sea Lord</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. In 1917 the British members renamed themselves Mountbatten.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battenberg-family.jpg" alt="Battenberg family" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NoahHK · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Battenberg-Mountbatten.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The House of Battenberg was a non-dynastic cadet branch of the House of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, which ruled the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> until 1918. Its first member was Countess Julia von Hauke, the orphaned daughter of Count Moritz von Hauke, a Polish nobleman who had served as a general in the Imperial Russian Army. She married Prince Alexander of Hesse, the third son of Grand Duke Louis II, in a morganatic marriage. Her rank was too low for their children to qualify for the succession, so Grand Duke Louis III created the title of Countess of Battenberg for her and her descendants in 1851.</p><p>The name refers to the town of Battenberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, and a previous family of counts of that name had become extinct in the 14th century. In 1858 the title was raised to princely status, with the style of Serene Highness. There was never a principality of Battenberg, and the family had no right of succession to the Grand Duchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The couple's eldest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-louis-of-battenberg">Prince Louis of Battenberg</a>, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sea-lord">First Sea Lord</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. In 1879 Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-of-battenberg">Alexander of Battenberg</a> was elected ruling Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and he was forced to abdicate in 1886. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry</a> of Battenberg married Princess Beatrice, the youngest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, and their daughter Victoria Eugenie became queen consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> after Edward VII raised her style to Royal Highness. In 1897 Prince Francis Joseph married Princess Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>.</p><p>Louis's daughter Alice married Prince Andrew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and their son, Prince Philip, took the surname Mountbatten and married the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a>. Louis's daughter Louise married the future Gustaf VI Adolf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1923.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1917, with anti-German feeling rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, most of the family, then living in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, renounced their Hessian titles and changed the name to Mountbatten, an anglicised version of Battenberg. Prince Louis became the first Marquess of Milford Haven and Prince Alexander, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s eldest son, Marquess of Carisbrooke, both peerages granted by their cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a>. Louis's younger son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten">Louis Mountbatten</a>, became Earl Mountbatten of Burma and the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy of India</a>. Juan, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, son of Victoria Eugenie, bore the surname Borbón y Battenberg until his death in 1993, and Prince Francis Joseph, who died childless in 1924, was the last born member to keep the name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battenberg family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A morganatic branch, the <b>Battenbergs</b>, produced Alexander, the first Prince of Bulgaria, and Louis of Battenberg, First Sea Lord of the Royal Navy, whose descendants took the name Mountbatten.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
